r the  story are used mostly to keep the sales  up instead of servicing said story it  gets readers invested when they learned  that the normal everyday life of their  favorite hero or villain is about to be  disrupted in a crazy way it makes  for some really great attention-grabbing  clickbait headlines or maybe a really  wacky cover for the issue that sells  particularly well but these major  changes don't stay for long they're an  easily reversible gimmick it takes an  insane amount of work care and  persistence to make any big character  growth stay that's not to say it never  happens the depressed loner Batman one  day decided to take on a warden  basically became the adoptive father of  a whole pantheon of kids meaning he's  not really as lonely as we all used to  think he is spider-man was even able to  grow up beyond his teenage superhero  schtick and is now a functional adult  who pays his own bills he was the  original teen superhero but for all  these other copycats granted both of  those examples happen in the 40s and 60s  before these characters became too  popular for their own good  I suppose Hank Pym isn't popular enough  to keep stagnant he's Ultron now and  that's been around for quite a while I  hope that sticks you could say it  happened with venom slowly but surely he  stopped being a poorly written edgy 90s  spider-man villain and started being a  sci-fi horror themed antihero that  Spidey considers a friend magneto is no  longer the violent terrorist  supervillain that he used to be and that  was a cherished ally and friend of the  x-men to honor the legacy of Professor X  who will come back to life as soon as  the Marvel Studios movie comes out and  magneto will become evil again because  brand synergy jeez I'm not helping my  point but it happens sometimes  yet in the case of characters like the  lizard you can't help but feel bad about  how it's always the same old problems on  repeat with brief intermissions of  something unique before it all snaps  back into place it's a special no-win  scenario for creatives when they're  making these characters because you run  the risk of breaking a character someone  else created decades ago and stamping on  their ideas or by making a change that's  so permanent in airtight that the next  guy feels creatively stifled because he  can't change it if you just write  whatever you want fans be damned it's  easy to completely alienate your  audience and destroy their interest in  the story if you write solely based on  whatever the fans want it turns into a  shallow mess of  and service and nostalgia baiting with  no clear direction I'm not sure what  solution there is here what position to  take because it's such a complicated  problem  lizards syndrome is a flaw that sort of  comes with the territory for this  creative medium though sometimes I see  example of lizard syndrome and other art  mediums where storytellers are so  pressured by massive almost religiously  devout phantoms that they have to play  it incredibly safe and try to keep the  series fresh by taking risks it's a  clumsy tightrope walk that if you can  make it across usually leaving this  middling effect where growth happens but  doesn't matter five minutes later take  Star Wars for example they pulled a  major plot twist in the second movie of  this new trilogy by killing off the main  villain completely throwing out  expectations of the series just  rehashing the old movies then that twist  left a very vocal set of fans feeling  dissatisfied and disappointed and  discouraged and disjointed disc and here  we are with a sequel to retcon Snoke  into being like just a puppet like a  literal meat puppet possessed by the fan  favorite villain who is behind  everything even though that makes zero  sense the fandom hated a major change so  the writers panicked and undid it making  the whole thing feel even less  satisfying I know a lot of people are up  in arms about the Palpatine situation  but this sort of thing happens on a  weekly basis if you're reading comic  books in fact this may as well have been  the ending of the Clone Saga where they  just said oh yeah Norman Osborn is  behind everything even though we killed  him off decades ago let's let's let's  wrap this up because we wrote ourselves  into a corner fans are nearly impossible  to please but that doesn't stop writers  from trying their best to do it anyway  with varying results I'd like to open up  the conversation to you the viewer and  ask what do you think would be the best  way to work around this lizard syndrome  problem so that both creators and the  fans can be happy while still letting  heroes and villains evolve organically  and try new things is there an answer to  this paradox well maybe you're a writer  Marvel listening to this and you've just  been inspired to try your own method  either way good ol Curt Connors is here  to stay and he's far from the worst case  of repetitive characters and comics but  a part of me does wish that the guy  could finally get a proper happy ending  [Music]  [Music]
 

